<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wang Jian (Qin)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wang Jian was a general of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> under King Zheng, the future First Emperor. He conquered Zhao and, after an earlier Qin army had failed, led a force of some 600,000 men against Chu, destroying it in 223 BCE. His victories were central to the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 221 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wang-jian-qin.jpg" alt="Wang Jian (Qin)" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%A7%A6%E5%A4%A7%E5%B0%86%E5%86%9B%E7%8E%8B%E7%BF%A6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wang Jian was born in Dongxiang, Pinyang, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a>, north-east of present-day Fuping County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-book-of-tang">New Book of Tang</a> his family's original surname was Ji, and his 16th-generation ancestor in the male line was Prince Qiao, a son of King Ling of Zhou, who was reduced to commoner status for remonstrating with his father; because people still saw his family as royal, they called it the Wang, the king's family, and his descendants took that surname.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 236 BCE Wang Jian led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> army that attacked Zhao and took nine cities, the start of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Ying Zheng</a>'s wars of unification. Zhao ceased to exist in 228 BCE, after Qin spies at the Zhao court had its leading general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-mu">Li Mu</a> replaced and Wang Jian captured its king. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jing-ke">Jing Ke</a>'s failed attempt to assassinate the king, Wang Jian conquered Jing Ke's home state of Yan in 226 BCE.</p><p>Against Chu, Wang Jian asked for 600,000 men, while the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-xin-qin">Li Xin</a> said 200,000 would do. The king chose Li Xin, and Wang Jian retired, pleading ill health. Li Xin's army was destroyed at the Battle of Chengfu by Chu forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yan">Xiang Yan</a> and Lord Changping, and the king recalled Wang Jian with the army he had asked for. At the send-off, and several times during the campaign, he demanded lavish rewards; he later told his men that the distrustful king would think a general with such petty wants had no designs on the throne.</p><p>From 224 BCE Wang Jian kept his troops inside their fortifications, training them in secret, until after a year Chu disbanded most of its army. He then overran Huaiyang, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yan">Xiang Yan</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shouchun">Shouchun</a>, and Xiang Yan died at Qinan, killed in battle or by his own hand. Lord Changping was killed by Wang Jian's deputy Meng Wu, and Chu fell in 223 BCE. Letters home from two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> soldiers, Hei Fu and Jin, asking for clothing and money, record the long campaign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Wang Jian later retired because of old age. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-ben">Wang Ben</a>, himself an important general, finished the conquests against Yan, Dai and Qi, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> in 221 BCE. The Wang clans of Langya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a> counted Wang Jian among their ancestors, and modern Chinese folklore ranks him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bai-qi">Bai Qi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lian-po">Lian Po</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-mu">Li Mu</a> as one of the four foremost generals of the late Warring States.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wang Jian (Qin)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 225 BCE Chu routed a Qin army under Li Xin, but the following year <b>Wang Jian</b> invaded with a reported 600,000 men.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After a failed siege of Handan in 259–257 BCE, between 230 and 221 BCE King Zheng's generals, among them <b>Wang Jian</b>, conquered the six remaining states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Military</a></li></ul></section>
